    {
      "path": "palette-probe.asm",
      "name": "palette-probe.asm",
      "kind": "source",
      "source": ".include \"web64/cartridge.inc\"\n.include \"cartridge/generated.inc\"\n\n; The C entry has already initialized the managed directory. These generated\n; handles are build-local; the application never hard-codes a physical bank.\nasm_palette_probe:\n_asm_palette_probe:\n    web64_rt_call_cart_resource_read asm_request, asm_result\n    rts\n\nasm_request:\n    .byte WEB64_CART_RESOURCE_PALETTE, 0, 0, 0, 0\n    .word $5004\n    .word 4\nasm_result:\n    .word 0\n"
    },
    {
      "path": "README.md",
      "name": "README.md",
      "kind": "source",
      "source": "# Native managed cartridge runtime\n\nThis IDE-saved project has one resident C Build Target and four native cartridge layouts.\nChoose a layout in Disk/Media → Cartridge Layouts, then Run Cartridge. The program\nreads the logical palette resource and changes the C64 border/background. The\napplication source names no bank, window or hardware profile. Build Cartridge\ncreates cartridge/generated.h and cartridge/generated.inc as inspectable\nprojections; they are not files to edit or carry outside this project.\n\nCartridge autostart runs before KERNAL screen setup. The C entry therefore\nselects VIC bank 0 and the default text screen/charset, enables the display,\nclears Screen and Color RAM, and then reads the palette. A blue border without\nvisible text means the display initialization did not run; inspecting screen\nRAM alone is not enough to verify the demo.\n\nThe palette occupies four stored ROM bytes. To exercise multiple extents,\nimport a larger binary file into the project and select it as a logical\nresource; Standard 8K and Standard 16K have much lower capacity than Magic\nDesk and EasyFlash. Transfer buffers are explicitly reserved at $5000-$50ff.\nThe resource directory is copied to $4000. The resident program loads at\n$2000. Every profile starts from an actual CRT boot, including EasyFlash's\nROMH reset trampoline and pinned ROML payload.\n\nmain.c calls the C resource API and then palette-probe.asm calls the same\nlogical API through web64/cartridge.inc. The successful screen displays\nWEB64 ASM. The assembly routine owns its packed request/result storage,\ncopies into the second reserved buffer at $5004 and returns status in A.\n\nDo not use F5 to launch the resident target as an ordinary PRG. Use Run\nCartridge (Ctrl+Alt+F5) so the cartridge mapping and directory exist. Interrupt\nhandlers must preserve $fb-$fe, stay valid during mapping transitions and must\nnot call the cartridge API. Managed banking does not intercept raw writes to\n$de00/$de02 or bank arbitrary executable code.\n"
    }
  ]
